{"name":"Mary Rose","hex":"#d7b1b0","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"mary","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Alternative letter-case form of Mary (“male homosexual”)."]},{"part":"Interjection","definitions":["Alternative form of marry (“indeed, in truth”)."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/mary"},{"term":"rose","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A shrub of the genus Rosa, with red, pink, white or yellow flowers.","A flower of the rose plant."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To make rose-colored; to redden or flush.","To perfume, as with roses."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/rose"}],"entries":[{"title":"Mary Rose","blocks":[{"text":"The Mary Rose was a carrack in the English Tudor navy of King Henry VIII. She was launched in 1511 and served for 34 years in several wars against France, Scotland, and Brittany. After being substantially rebuilt in 1536, she saw her last action on 19 July 1545. She led the attack on the galleys of a French invasion fleet, but sank off Spithead in the Solent, the strait north of the Isle of Wight."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5d/AnthonyRoll-2_Mary_Rose.jpg/330px-AnthonyRoll-2_Mary_Rose.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mary_Rose","wikidata":"Q544894"},{"title":"Mary Rose (play)","blocks":[{"text":"Mary Rose is a play by J. M. Barrie, who is best known for Peter Pan. It was first produced in April 1920 at the Haymarket Theatre, London, with incidental music specially composed by Norman O'Neill. The play was produced in New York that year. Its most recent revival in New York was in 2007 and in London in 2012."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/a/ab/Ruth_Chatterton_%28May_1921%29.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mary_Rose_(play)","wikidata":"Q6780636"},{"title":"Rose","blocks":[{"text":"A rose is either a woody perennial flowering plant of the genus Rosa, in the family Rosaceae, or the flower it bears. There are over three hundred species and tens of thousands of cultivars. The plants range in size and form, from trailing and erect shrubs to climbers up to 7 metres (23 ft) in height. Sharp prickles appear on the stems of many species. Their flowers vary in size and shape and are usually large and showy, in colours ranging from white through pinks, reds, oranges and yellows."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e6/Rosa_rubiginosa_1.jpg/330px-Rosa_rubiginosa_1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rose","wikidata":"Q34687"},{"title":"Rose (color)","blocks":[{"text":"Rose is the color halfway between red and magenta, whose hue corresponds to an angle of 330 degrees on the HSV/HSL color wheel."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2c/Pink-Rose-Sitia-Crete.jpg/330px-Pink-Rose-Sitia-Crete.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rose_(color)","wikidata":"Q533047"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ec/Henry_VIII_%28reigned_1509-1547%29_by_English_School.jpg/500px-Henry_VIII_%28reigned_1509-1547%29_by_English_School.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ec/Henry_VIII_%28reigned_1509-1547%29_by_English_School.jpg/1280px-Henry_VIII_%28reigned_1509-1547%29_by_English_School.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ec/Henry_VIII_%28reigned_1509-1547%29_by_English_School.jpg/500px-Henry_VIII_%28reigned_1509-1547%29_by_English_School.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Portrait of Henry VIII in 1509, aged c.18, the year of his coronation, by Meynnart Wewyck","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AHenry_VIII_(reigned_1509-1547)_by_English_School.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bb/The_Embarkation_of_Henry_VIII_at_Dover.jpg/500px-The_Embarkation_of_Henry_VIII_at_Dover.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bb/The_Embarkation_of_Henry_VIII_at_Dover.jpg/1280px-The_Embarkation_of_Henry_VIII_at_Dover.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bb/The_Embarkation_of_Henry_VIII_at_Dover.jpg/500px-The_Embarkation_of_Henry_VIII_at_Dover.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The Embarkation of Henry VIII at Dover, a painting that commemorated King Henry's voyage to the Field of the Cloth of Gold in 1520, painted in 1540. The vessels in the painting are shown decorated with wooden panels similar to those that would have been used on the Mary Rose on special occasions.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AThe_Embarkation_of_Henry_VIII_at_Dover.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/af/MaryRose-ship_hall.jpg/500px-MaryRose-ship_hall.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/af/MaryRose-ship_hall.jpg/500px-MaryRose-ship_hall.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The remains of the Mary Rose's hull. All deck levels can be made out clearly, including the minor remnants of the sterncastle deck.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AMaryRose-ship_hall.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3b/MaryRose-rigging_blocks2.JPG/500px-MaryRose-